# Code owners for estamora-conformance-spec.
#
# The runner repository gained this file because its `SECURITY.md` sends a reporter to
# "a maintainer listed in `CODEOWNERS`" and no such file existed. This repository's
# `SECURITY.md` names a maintainer through the affected profile's `profile.maintainers`
# field instead, so this file is not closing a broken reference here — it is here so that
# the two repositories answer the same question the same way, and so that GitHub asks the
# right person to review a change that alters what conformance means.
#
# That distinction is the reason the paths below are what they are. In the runner
# repository the load-bearing files are code and supply-chain declarations. Here they are
# normative *documents*: a schema, a profile, or a vector is not an implementation of a
# requirement, it is the requirement. A change to one of them changes what every
# conformance result produced against it means, and a change to a published profile version
# is governed by `VERSIONING.md` and `GOVERNANCE.md` rather than by review alone.

# The ten normative JSON Schemas. Each rejects unknown properties, so these documents
# decide which requirements can be expressed at all — a requirement with no schema support
# cannot be written down.
/schema/                                       @winningtalker-commits

# The published profiles. A version directory here is what a receipt names, which is why a
# published version is never edited in place and a correction is a new version with
# `supersedes` set.
/profiles/                                     @winningtalker-commits

# The shared vector corpus. A vector is a specification artefact authored by a person, and
# a vector that states the wrong expectation invalidates every result measured with it.
/vectors/                                      @winningtalker-commits

# Canonical identifiers are load-bearing: a `$ref` between schemas resolves against the
# `$id` of the document containing it, so a wrong base makes the schemas unreadable from
# outside a clone. That defect shipped once already, and these two files are what caught it
# and what publishes the documents it names.
/scripts/verify-published-schemas.py           @winningtalker-commits
/scripts/build-docs-site.sh                    @winningtalker-commits

# The validation tooling decides whether a defective profile passes validation, which is
# the one failure mode this repository exists to prevent.
/scripts/                                      @winningtalker-commits
/tests/                                        @winningtalker-commits

# The rules themselves, which a profile change is answerable to.
/VERSIONING.md                                 @winningtalker-commits
/GOVERNANCE.md                                 @winningtalker-commits
/SECURITY.md                                   @winningtalker-commits
/.github/CODEOWNERS                            @winningtalker-commits

# The release and deployment plumbing, whose mistakes are the ones that cannot be undone.
/.github/workflows/                            @winningtalker-commits

# Default owner. The organisation has a single maintainer today; the normative paths above
# are the ones to split first, because a change to one of them changes what conformance
# means rather than how it is checked.
*                                              @winningtalker-commits
